   "Check the bottom of the coil and the area right above the drain pan, where dirt and biofilm collect first.",
   "Look for a filter bypass path that let unfiltered air reach the coil: gaps at the filter rack, an undersized filter, or an open cabinet seam.",
   "If the coil is loaded, plan a proper cleaning that reaches the entering face, which usually means removing the coil or the access panel rather than spraying through the leaving side.",
   "Inspect the drain pan and drain while the coil is exposed, since the same debris ends up there.",
   "After cleaning, retake the coil pressure drop and total external static and compare to your starting numbers.",
   "Fix the filtration bypass that caused it, or you will be back."
