  "summary": "Not every blown control fuse is a shorted thermostat wire. A solenoid coil that is energized but mechanically prevented from pulling in never develops its normal inductive reactance, so it draws far more current than it should - the coil version of locked rotor. A welded contactor, a reversing valve coil that is not seated on its stem, or a jammed damper motor can eat control fuses with no wiring fault at all.",
