  "title": "Blower or board acts on a call that is not there - checking off-state leakage from the thermostat output",
  "summary": "Electronic thermostats switch their outputs with triacs, and a triac that is off still leaks a small current. A high-impedance input such as an ECM control, a variable-speed board, or a solid-state relay can read that leakage as a real call, and a small mechanical relay may chatter instead of dropping out cleanly. A meter across an open triac output often reads close to full 24V, which fools people into thinking there is a genuine call.",
